[2026-03-15T23:24:01.772Z] {"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01K9tp5p2zonyWBHAcny3s3t","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→# Skill\n     2→\n     3→You will run all the tests in the app and get them to pass..\n     4→\n     5→## Unpack Subtasks\n     6→\n     7→List all test files in the app's `tests/` directory. Add ALL test file tasks in a single\n     8→`add-task` call, one task per test file in alphabetical order. Write out every task\n     9→explicitly — do not use a loop or script:\n    10→\n    11→```bash\n    12→npx tsx /repo/scripts/add-task.ts <<'EOF'\n    13→[\n    14→  { \"skill\": \"skills/tasks/build/testing.md\", \"app\": \"<AppName>\", \"subtasks\": [\"FixTests: tests/<file1>.spec.ts\"] },\n    15→  { \"skill\": \"skills/tasks/build/testing.md\", \"app\": \"<AppName>\", \"subtasks\": [\"FixTests: tests/<file2>.spec.ts\"] }\n    16→]\n    17→EOF\n    18→```\n    19→\n    20→After running tests and there are failures, pick specific failing tests and add a task to fix\n    21→them without regressing any tests that passed in previous runs:\n    22→\n    23→```bash\n    24→npx tsx /repo/scripts/add-task.ts <<'EOF'\n    25→[{ \"skill\": \"skills/tasks/build/testing.md\", \"app\": \"<AppName>\", \"subtasks\": [\"Fix: <failing test name 1>\", \"Fix: <failing test name 2>\"] }]\n    26→EOF\n    27→```\n    28→\n    29→## Requirements\n    30→\n    31→Once all playwright tests have been written, you must get them to pass.\n    32→When getting tests to pass, you must ensure the app is actually working.\n    33→You must not paper over problems in the app by hard-coding values,\n    34→disabling test functionality, and so on.\n    35→\n    36→You might encounter bugs in the tests and can fix those, but you must ensure\n    37→that the test is correctly exercising the app as described in the corresponding\n    38→entry in tests.md\n    39→\n    40→You MUST read these skill files before testing.\n    41→\n    42→https://raw.githubusercontent.com/replayio/skills/refs/heads/main/skills/replay-cli/SKILL.md\n    43→https://raw.githubusercontent.com/replayio/skills/refs/heads/main/skills/replay-mcp/SKILL.md\n    44→\n    45→RECORD_REPLAY_API_KEY is already set in the environment for using the Replay CLI.\n    46→\n    47→## Replay.io Playwright Installation and Configuration\n    48→\n    49→Every app MUST use the Replay browser to record Playwright test runs. This section describes\n    50→the exact installation steps and configuration required.\n    51→\n    52→### 1. Install dependencies\n    53→\n    54→The app's `package.json` must include these dev dependencies:\n    55→\n    56→```bash\n    57→npm install --save-dev @replayio/playwright @playwright/test\n    58→```\n    59→\n    60→`@replayio/playwright` (currently v5.0.1) provides the `replayReporter` and `devices` exports\n    61→for integrating Replay recordings into Playwright.\n    62→\n    63→### 2. Install the Replay browser\n    64→\n    65→Run once in the container (or as part of CI setup):\n    66→\n    67→```bash\n    68→npx replayio install\n    69→```\n    70→\n    71→This downloads the Replay Chromium browser to `~/.replay/runtimes/chrome-linux/chrome`.\n    72→Verify it exists:\n    73→\n    74→```bash\n    75→ls ~/.replay/runtimes/chrome-linux/chrome\n    76→```\n    77→\n    78→### 3. API key\n    79→\n    80→`RECORD_REPLAY_API_KEY` must be set in the environment. It is already available in this\n    81→container. The Playwright config passes it to the reporter via:\n    82→\n    83→```ts\n    84→apiKey: process.env.REPLAY_API_KEY ?? process.env.RECORD_REPLAY_API_KEY\n    85→```\n    86→\n    87→### 4. Playwright config\n    88→\n    89→The Playwright config MUST:\n    90→\n    91→1. Import BOTH `replayReporter` and `devices as replayDevices` from `@replayio/playwright`.\n    92→2. Include `replayReporter(...)` in the `reporter` array with `upload: false`. Uploads are\n    93→   handled by the test script, NOT by the reporter.\n    94→3. Spread `replayDevices['Replay Chromium']` into the global `use` config. This sets\n    95→   `executablePath`, `RECORD_ALL_CONTENT`, and critically `RECORD_REPLAY_METADATA_FILE` which\n    96→   is required for the reporter to attach test metadata (pass/fail result) to recordings.\n    97→   Without `RECORD_REPLAY_METADATA_FILE`, the recording driver cannot read the per-test\n    98→   metadata written by the fixture, and all recordings will have empty `testResult`.\n    99→\n   100→**IMPORTANT**: Do NOT manually set `launchOptions.executablePath` and `env` instead of using\n   101→`replayDevices['Replay Chromium']`. Manual config misses `RECORD_REPLAY_METADATA_FILE`,\n   102→which breaks the recording-to-test metadata association.\n   103→\n   104→**IMPORTANT**: The Replay Chromium browser adds instrumentation overhead that makes all\n   105→operations ~2–3x slower than standard Chromium. Always set higher timeouts from the start:\n   106→\n   107→- `actionTimeout: 15000` (15s for clicks, fills, etc.)\n   108→- `navigationTimeout: 30000` (30s for page navigations)\n   109→- Use `expect.toBeVisible({ timeout: 10000 })` for post-navigation assertions\n   110→\n   111→Example:\n   112→\n   113→```ts\n   114→import { defineConfig, devices } from '@playwright/test';\n   115→import { devices as replayDevices, replayReporter } from '@replayio/playwright';\n   116→\n   117→export default defineConfig({\n   118→  // Replay Chromium is slower due to instrumentation — use generous timeouts\n   119→  actionTimeout: 15000,\n   120→  navigationTimeout: 30000,\n   121→  reporter: [\n   122→    replayReporter({\n   123→      apiKey: process.env.REPLAY_API_KEY ?? process.env.RECORD_REPLAY_API_KEY,\n   124→      upload: false,\n   125→    }),\n   126→    ['json', { outputFile: 'test-results/results.json' }],\n   127→    ['list'],\n   128→  ],\n   129→  use: {\n   130→    ...replayDevices['Replay Chromium'],\n   131→  },\n   132→});\n   133→```\n   134→\n   135→### 5. Replay CLI commands reference\n   136→\n   137→- `npx replayio list` — List all local recordings (ID, host, date, duration, status).\n   138→- `npx replayio list --json` — Full JSON metadata for all recordings. Each entry includes:\n   139→  `id`, `buildId`, `date`, `duration`, `metadata` (host, URI, sourceMaps, processType),\n   140→  `path`, `recordingStatus` (one of `\"recording\"`, `\"finished\"`, `\"crashed\"`).\n   141→- `npx replayio upload <id>` — Upload a single recording by ID. Returns the viewable URL.\n   142→- `npx replayio upload --all` — Upload all local recordings.\n   143→- `npx replayio remove --all` — Delete all local recordings (use between runs to avoid stale\n   144→  data accumulating).\n   145→\n   146→### 6. OpenSSL 1.1 requirement\n   147→\n   148→The Replay browser's recording driver requires `libcrypto.so.1.1` (OpenSSL 1.1). If the\n   149→container only has OpenSSL 3, the browser will run but silently fail to record (no error in\n   150→test output, just `DoLoadDriverHandle: dlopen failed` in stderr). Fix: download Ubuntu 18.04's\n   151→`libssl1.1` deb, extract it, and set `LD_LIBRARY_PATH` to point at the extracted libs. See\n   152→the deployment Playwright config for a working example.\n   153→\n   154→### 7. Pre-test verification\n   155→\n   156→Before running tests for the first time in an app, verify the Replay browser is installed:\n   157→\n   158→```bash\n   159→ls ~/.replay/runtimes/chrome-linux/chrome\n   160→```\n   161→\n   162→If it does not exist, run `npx replayio install` before any test execution. Running tests\n   163→without the Replay browser means failures produce no recordings, making debugging impossible.\n   164→Do not defer Replay installation to after failures are discovered.\n   165→\n   166→## Pre-Flight Checks\n   167→\n   168→Before running any tests, run the standard pre-flight procedure from `skills/scripts/preflight.md`.\n   169→Run each preflight step as a **separate command** — do NOT chain them with `&&` or `;` in a\n   170→single command. Chained commands fail if any sub-command fails (especially `pkill` when no\n   171→process exists), producing confusing output and wasting effort decomposing and re-running.\n   172→\n   173→```bash\n   174→# Step 1: Kill stale servers (including zombies holding port 8888)\n   175→pkill -f \"netlify|vite\" 2>/dev/null || true\n   176→# If port 8888 is still occupied after pkill (zombie processes invisible to lsof),\n   177→# find PIDs via /proc/net/tcp6 and kill -9 them:\n   178→fuser -k 8888/tcp 2>/dev/null || true\n   179→```\n   180→```bash\n   181→# Step 2: Verify env vars\n   182→grep NEON_PROJECT_ID .env || echo \"ERROR: NEON_PROJECT_ID not set\"\n   183→```\n   184→```bash\n   185→# Step 3: Verify Replay browser\n   186→ls ~/.replay/runtimes/chrome-linux/chrome 2>/dev/null || npx replayio install\n   187→```\n   188→```bash\n   189→# Step 4: Verify dependencies\n   190→ls node_modules/@neondatabase/serverless 2>/dev/null || npm install --legacy-peer-deps\n   191→```\n   192→```bash\n   193→# Step 5: Clear stale recordings\n   194→npx replayio remove --all 2>/dev/null\n   195→```\n   196→\n   197→See `skills/scripts/preflight.md` for the full pre-flight checklist and\n   198→`skills/scripts/env-setup.md` for environment prerequisites.\n   199→\n   200→## Running Tests"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"1880e500-2182-4a27-9a5d-5f74c5e8203c","uuid":"3f53f834-35c0-46a4-bc36-cad4436899fc","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/repo/skills/tasks/build/testing.md","content":"# Skill\n\nYou will run all the tests in the app and get them to pass..\n\n## Unpack Subtasks\n\nList all test files in the app's `tests/` directory. Add ALL test file tasks in a single\n`add-task` call, one task per test file in alphabetical order. Write out every task\nexplicitly — do not use a loop or script:\n\n```bash\nnpx tsx /repo/scripts/add-task.ts <<'EOF'\n[\n  { \"skill\": \"skills/tasks/build/testing.md\", \"app\": \"<AppName>\", \"subtasks\": [\"FixTests: tests/<file1>.spec.ts\"] },\n  { \"skill\": \"skills/tasks/build/testing.md\", \"app\": \"<AppName>\", \"subtasks\": [\"FixTests: tests/<file2>.spec.ts\"] }\n]\nEOF\n```\n\nAfter running tests and there are failures, pick specific failing tests and add a task to fix\nthem without regressing any tests that passed in previous runs:\n\n```bash\nnpx tsx /repo/scripts/add-task.ts <<'EOF'\n[{ \"skill\": \"skills/tasks/build/testing.md\", \"app\": \"<AppName>\", \"subtasks\": [\"Fix: <failing test name 1>\", \"Fix: <failing test name 2>\"] }]\nEOF\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nOnce all playwright tests have been written, you must get them to pass.\nWhen getting tests to pass, you must ensure the app is actually working.\nYou must not paper over problems in the app by hard-coding values,\ndisabling test functionality, and so on.\n\nYou might encounter bugs in the tests and can fix those, but you must ensure\nthat the test is correctly exercising the app as described in the corresponding\nentry in tests.md\n\nYou MUST read these skill files before testing.\n\nhttps://raw.githubusercontent.com/replayio/skills/refs/heads/main/skills/replay-cli/SKILL.md\nhttps://raw.githubusercontent.com/replayio/skills/refs/heads/main/skills/replay-mcp/SKILL.md\n\nRECORD_REPLAY_API_KEY is already set in the environment for using the Replay CLI.\n\n## Replay.io Playwright Installation and Configuration\n\nEvery app MUST use the Replay browser to record Playwright test runs. This section describes\nthe exact installation steps and configuration required.\n\n### 1. Install dependencies\n\nThe app's `package.json` must include these dev dependencies:\n\n```bash\nnpm install --save-dev @replayio/playwright @playwright/test\n```\n\n`@replayio/playwright` (currently v5.0.1) provides the `replayReporter` and `devices` exports\nfor integrating Replay recordings into Playwright.\n\n### 2. Install the Replay browser\n\nRun once in the container (or as part of CI setup):\n\n```bash\nnpx replayio install\n```\n\nThis downloads the Replay Chromium browser to `~/.replay/runtimes/chrome-linux/chrome`.\nVerify it exists:\n\n```bash\nls ~/.replay/runtimes/chrome-linux/chrome\n```\n\n### 3. API key\n\n`RECORD_REPLAY_API_KEY` must be set in the environment. It is already available in this\ncontainer. The Playwright config passes it to the reporter via:\n\n```ts\napiKey: process.env.REPLAY_API_KEY ?? process.env.RECORD_REPLAY_API_KEY\n```\n\n### 4. Playwright config\n\nThe Playwright config MUST:\n\n1. Import BOTH `replayReporter` and `devices as replayDevices` from `@replayio/playwright`.\n2. Include `replayReporter(...)` in the `reporter` array with `upload: false`. Uploads are\n   handled by the test script, NOT by the reporter.\n3. Spread `replayDevices['Replay Chromium']` into the global `use` config. This sets\n   `executablePath`, `RECORD_ALL_CONTENT`, and critically `RECORD_REPLAY_METADATA_FILE` which\n   is required for the reporter to attach test metadata (pass/fail result) to recordings.\n   Without `RECORD_REPLAY_METADATA_FILE`, the recording driver cannot read the per-test\n   metadata written by the fixture, and all recordings will have empty `testResult`.\n\n**IMPORTANT**: Do NOT manually set `launchOptions.executablePath` and `env` instead of using\n`replayDevices['Replay Chromium']`. Manual config misses `RECORD_REPLAY_METADATA_FILE`,\nwhich breaks the recording-to-test metadata association.\n\n**IMPORTANT**: The Replay Chromium browser adds instrumentation overhead that makes all\noperations ~2–3x slower than standard Chromium. Always set higher timeouts from the start:\n\n- `actionTimeout: 15000` (15s for clicks, fills, etc.)\n- `navigationTimeout: 30000` (30s for page navigations)\n- Use `expect.toBeVisible({ timeout: 10000 })` for post-navigation assertions\n\nExample:\n\n```ts\nimport { defineConfig, devices } from '@playwright/test';\nimport { devices as replayDevices, replayReporter } from '@replayio/playwright';\n\nexport default defineConfig({\n  // Replay Chromium is slower due to instrumentation — use generous timeouts\n  actionTimeout: 15000,\n  navigationTimeout: 30000,\n  reporter: [\n    replayReporter({\n      apiKey: process.env.REPLAY_API_KEY ?? process.env.RECORD_REPLAY_API_KEY,\n      upload: false,\n    }),\n    ['json', { outputFile: 'test-results/results.json' }],\n    ['list'],\n  ],\n  use: {\n    ...replayDevices['Replay Chromium'],\n  },\n});\n```\n\n### 5. Replay CLI commands reference\n\n- `npx replayio list` — List all local recordings (ID, host, date, duration, status).\n- `npx replayio list --json` — Full JSON metadata for all recordings. Each entry includes:\n  `id`, `buildId`, `date`, `duration`, `metadata` (host, URI, sourceMaps, processType),\n  `path`, `recordingStatus` (one of `\"recording\"`, `\"finished\"`, `\"crashed\"`).\n- `npx replayio upload <id>` — Upload a single recording by ID. Returns the viewable URL.\n- `npx replayio upload --all` — Upload all local recordings.\n- `npx replayio remove --all` — Delete all local recordings (use between runs to avoid stale\n  data accumulating).\n\n### 6. OpenSSL 1.1 requirement\n\nThe Replay browser's recording driver requires `libcrypto.so.1.1` (OpenSSL 1.1). If the\ncontainer only has OpenSSL 3, the browser will run but silently fail to record (no error in\ntest output, just `DoLoadDriverHandle: dlopen failed` in stderr). Fix: download Ubuntu 18.04's\n`libssl1.1` deb, extract it, and set `LD_LIBRARY_PATH` to point at the extracted libs. See\nthe deployment Playwright config for a working example.\n\n### 7. Pre-test verification\n\nBefore running tests for the first time in an app, verify the Replay browser is installed:\n\n```bash\nls ~/.replay/runtimes/chrome-linux/chrome\n```\n\nIf it does not exist, run `npx replayio install` before any test execution. Running tests\nwithout the Replay browser means failures produce no recordings, making debugging impossible.\nDo not defer Replay installation to after failures are discovered.\n\n## Pre-Flight Checks\n\nBefore running any tests, run the standard pre-flight procedure from `skills/scripts/preflight.md`.\nRun each preflight step as a **separate command** — do NOT chain them with `&&` or `;` in a\nsingle command. Chained commands fail if any sub-command fails (especially `pkill` when no\nprocess exists), producing confusing output and wasting effort decomposing and re-running.\n\n```bash\n# Step 1: Kill stale servers (including zombies holding port 8888)\npkill -f \"netlify|vite\" 2>/dev/null || true\n# If port 8888 is still occupied after pkill (zombie processes invisible to lsof),\n# find PIDs via /proc/net/tcp6 and kill -9 them:\nfuser -k 8888/tcp 2>/dev/null || true\n```\n```bash\n# Step 2: Verify env vars\ngrep NEON_PROJECT_ID .env || echo \"ERROR: NEON_PROJECT_ID not set\"\n```\n```bash\n# Step 3: Verify Replay browser\nls ~/.replay/runtimes/chrome-linux/chrome 2>/dev/null || npx replayio install\n```\n```bash\n# Step 4: Verify dependencies\nls node_modules/@neondatabase/serverless 2>/dev/null || npm install --legacy-peer-deps\n```\n```bash\n# Step 5: Clear stale recordings\nnpx replayio remove --all 2>/dev/null\n```\n\nSee `skills/scripts/preflight.md` for the full pre-flight checklist and\n`skills/scripts/env-setup.md` for environment prerequisites.\n\n## Running Tests","numLines":200,"startLine":1,"totalLines":924}}}
