[2026-03-16T00:07:42.156Z] {"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_019rLJcUP1g66ptE6HVNYC83","type":"tool_result","content":"    70→};\n    71→\n    72→export const fetchCycles = createAsyncThunk(\n    73→  'cycles/fetchCycles',\n    74→  async (teamId: string, { getState, rejectWithValue }) => {\n    75→    const state = getState() as { auth: { token: string | null } };\n    76→    const token = state.auth.token;\n    77→    if (!token) return rejectWithValue('No token');\n    78→    const response = await fetch(`/api/cycles?teamId=${teamId}`, {\n    79→      headers: { Authorization: `Bearer ${token}` },\n    80→    });\n    81→    if (!response.ok) {\n    82→      return rejectWithValue('Failed to fetch cycles');\n    83→    }\n    84→    return await response.json() as { cycles: CycleSummary[] };\n    85→  }\n    86→);\n    87→\n    88→export const fetchCycleIssues = createAsyncThunk(\n    89→  'cycles/fetchCycleIssues',\n    90→  async (cycleId: string, { getState, rejectWithValue }) => {\n    91→    const state = getState() as { auth: { token: string | null } };\n    92→    const token = state.auth.token;\n    93→    if (!token) return rejectWithValue('No token');\n    94→    const response = await fetch(`/api/cycle-issues?cycleId=${cycleId}`, {\n    95→      headers: { Authorization: `Bearer ${token}` },\n    96→    });\n    97→    if (!response.ok) {\n    98→      return rejectWithValue('Failed to fetch cycle issues');\n    99→    }\n   100→    return await response.json() as {\n   101→      cycle: CycleDetail;\n   102→      issues: CycleIssue[];\n   103→      members: CycleDetailMember[];\n   104→      burndown: BurndownEntry[];\n   105→    };\n   106→  }\n   107→);\n   108→\n   109→export const createCycle = createAsyncThunk("}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"1880e500-2182-4a27-9a5d-5f74c5e8203c","uuid":"436ab883-1c4b-4746-af0e-d5ab1f31a223","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/repo/apps/LinearClone/src/slices/cyclesSlice.ts","content":"};\n\nexport const fetchCycles = createAsyncThunk(\n  'cycles/fetchCycles',\n  async (teamId: string, { getState, rejectWithValue }) => {\n    const state = getState() as { auth: { token: string | null } };\n    const token = state.auth.token;\n    if (!token) return rejectWithValue('No token');\n    const response = await fetch(`/api/cycles?teamId=${teamId}`, {\n      headers: { Authorization: `Bearer ${token}` },\n    });\n    if (!response.ok) {\n      return rejectWithValue('Failed to fetch cycles');\n    }\n    return await response.json() as { cycles: CycleSummary[] };\n  }\n);\n\nexport const fetchCycleIssues = createAsyncThunk(\n  'cycles/fetchCycleIssues',\n  async (cycleId: string, { getState, rejectWithValue }) => {\n    const state = getState() as { auth: { token: string | null } };\n    const token = state.auth.token;\n    if (!token) return rejectWithValue('No token');\n    const response = await fetch(`/api/cycle-issues?cycleId=${cycleId}`, {\n      headers: { Authorization: `Bearer ${token}` },\n    });\n    if (!response.ok) {\n      return rejectWithValue('Failed to fetch cycle issues');\n    }\n    return await response.json() as {\n      cycle: CycleDetail;\n      issues: CycleIssue[];\n      members: CycleDetailMember[];\n      burndown: BurndownEntry[];\n    };\n  }\n);\n\nexport const createCycle = createAsyncThunk(","numLines":40,"startLine":70,"totalLines":227}}}
[2026-03-16T00:08:03.100Z] {"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01WJmkRZoonGLPuWo1xfiRD1","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The `fetchCycleIssues` returns the cycle detail including `startDate` and `endDate`. These come from the cycle-issues API which we fixed.\n\n15 bars = 15 days. This is from March 10 to March 24 (the Sprint 12 cycle!). The test creates a new cycle and clicks on it, but maybe it's actually viewing the Sprint 12 cycle's detail instead.\n\nThis could happen if the `fetchCycleIssues` dispatched when clicking the new cycle returns the Sprint 12 cycle's data, or if the cycle selection reverts.\n\nWait - looking at `ActiveCycle.tsx` more carefully, there's this effect:\n```ts\nuseEffect(() => {\n  dispatch(setSelectedCycleId(null));\n}, [teamId, dispatch]);\n```\n\nThis clears the selected cycle when teamId changes. But the test navigates to the same team. When the page loads, cycles are fetched, and then:\n```ts\nuseEffect(() => {\n  if (cycles.length > 0 && !selectedCycleId) {\n    const activeCycle = cycles.find((c) => c.isActive);\n    if (activeCycle) {\n      dispatch(setSelectedCycleId(activeCycle.id));\n    }\n  }\n}, [cycles, selectedCycleId, dispatch]);\n```\n\nThis auto-selects the active cycle. Sprint 12 (Mar 10-24) would be active on Mar 16. So when the page loads, it auto-selects Sprint 12. Then the test clicks on the new cycle, which sets `selectedCycleId` to the new cycle's ID.\n\nBut if there's a race condition where the auto-select fires after the click... Actually, looking at this, the cycle list should render, then the test clicks the new cycle. The auto-select only fires when `!selectedCycleId`, so if the user already selected a cycle, it won't auto-select.\n\nWait - actually looking at the ActiveCycle effects more carefully:\n\n```ts\n// Clear selected cycle when team changes\nuseEffect(() => {\n  dispatch(setSelectedCycleId(null));\n}, [teamId, dispatch]);\n```\n\nThis runs on initial mount. Then:\n```ts\nuseEffect(() => {\n  if (cycles.length > 0 && !selectedCycleId) {\n    const activeCycle = cycles.find((c) => c.isActive);\n    if (activeCycle) dispatch(setSelectedCycleId(activeCycle.id));\n  }\n}, [cycles, selectedCycleId, dispatch]);\n```\n\nThis auto-selects the active cycle. When the test clicks the new cycle row, `handleSelectCycle` dispatches `setSelectedCycleId(newCycleId)`. Then `fetchCycleIssues(newCycleId)` is dispatched. But...\n\nCould it be that the auto-select fires again because of the useEffect dependency on `selectedCycleId`? When `selectedCycleId` changes to the new cycle's ID, the effect re-runs but `selectedCycleId` is now set, so the `if (!selectedCycleId)` check prevents re-selection.\n\nHmm, let me think about this differently. The 15 bars match the Sprint 12 cycle dates (Mar 10 to Mar 24 = 15 days). So somehow the chart is showing Sprint 12's data even though the test clicked on the new cycle.\n\nMaybe the `fetchCycleIssues` for the new cycle hasn't completed yet, so `cycleDetail` still contains Sprint 12's data. The test waits for `cycle-detail` to be visible but doesn't wait for the detail to update to the new cycle.\n\nActually wait - when the user clicks the new cycle:\n1. `setSelectedCycleId(newCycleId)` is dispatched\n2. The ActiveCycle component re-renders, showing `CycleDetail` if `selectedCycleId && cycleDetail`\n3. `cycleDetail` might still be Sprint 12's data (from auto-selection)\n4. `fetchCycleIssues(newCycleId)` is dispatched via the useEffect\n5. When that resolves, `cycleDetail` updates to the new cycle\n\nBut between steps 3 and 5, the chart shows Sprint 12's dates. The test checks `toHaveCount(28)` which times out because it sees 15 (Sprint 12's 15 days).\n\nThe fix should be to either:\n1. Wait for the detail to show the correct cycle name before checking bars\n2. Or clear `cycleDetail` when `selectedCycleId` changes\n\nLet me update the test to wait for the correct cycle name before checking the chart:"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":897,"cache_read_input_tokens":154035,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":897,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"1880e500-2182-4a27-9a5d-5f74c5e8203c","uuid":"03ae4b3d-f0af-4a30-9815-8957102c5d41"}
