   return await response.json() as {\n      cycle: CycleDetail;\n      issues: CycleIssue[];\n      members: CycleDetailMember[];\n      burndown: BurndownEntry[];\n    };\n  }\n);\n\nexport const createCycle = createAsyncThunk(\n  'cycles/createCycle',\n  async (\n    payload: { teamId: string; name: string; startDate: string; endDate: string },","numLines":25,"startLine":88,"totalLines":227}}}
[2026-03-16T00:26:52.318Z] {"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01KPaRbqAWNFL34C5VGGsXh7","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The approach should be: use the `signal` from `createAsyncThunk` to abort the fetch, and add a check in the `fetchCycleIssues` thunk. But `createAsyncThunk` already provides an `AbortController` signal via `thunkAPI.signal`. However, we need the component to abort the previous request when dispatching a new one.\n\nActually, a better approach: use the `meta.arg` (the cycleId) in the fulfilled handler to check against `selectedCycleId`. Let me look at how createAsyncThunk works - the `action.meta.arg` in the fulfilled case is the original argument.\n\n\nI'm checking if the cycleId passed to the thunk matches the currently selected cycle before updating the state, which prevents stale responses from overwriting newer data. But wait, this is essentially the same as comparing the cycle ID from the response payload itself, so either approach would work.\n\nThe real issue is that the ID check is somehow blocking cycleDetail from being populated when tests 4 and 7 run. Looking at the test failures in run-15, both tests timeout waiting for the cycle detail to appear, but in run-17 without the ID check, the detail loads fine. So something about the condition is preventing the state update for those specific cycles. Let me trace through what happens when test 4 loads an empty cycle—the page navigates, cycles load, Sprint 12 gets auto-selected, and then... The ID check in the reducer is the safeguard here—when the initial fetch completes, it won't store the result if the IDs don't match, keeping the cycle list visible. Once the user's click triggers a fetch for the empty cycle, that result will match and render the detail view. The 30-second timeout should be plenty of time unless that second fetch never actually fires.\n\nWait, I'm wondering if there's a React 18 batching issue at play. When the user clicks the cycle row, the state update gets batched, so the component doesn't immediately re-render. By the time the first fetch completes and the component finally re-renders with the new selected ID, the useEffect should trigger the second fetch—but maybe something's preventing that from happening.\n\nActually, I think I found the issue. The selector is destructuring the entire cycles object every render, which creates a new reference each time. This could be causing unnecessary re-renders or preventing the dependency array from detecting actual changes properly.\n\nThe useEffect should fire when selectedCycleId changes, but maybe I need to use abort signals in the thunk instead—createAsyncThunk supports this with thunkAPI.signal, so when a new request comes in, it can cancel the previous one.\n\nActually, let me keep this simpler: I'll store the dispatch result in the useEffect and call abort() on cleanup if a new cycle is selected before the previous fetch completes.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":643,"cache_read_input_tokens":85420,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":643,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":9,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"1880e500-2182-4a27-9a5d-5f74c5e8203c","uuid":"af4ae7fc-6955-459c-982e-4833b0855b4b"}
