ail` helper to handle the case where CycleDetail is already showing with an auto-selected cycle, and handle the race condition at the test level for tests 4 and 7.\n\nActually, let me step back. The abort approach works theoretically but something's broken in practice. Let me revert and focus on a minimal fix. Looking at the original state without abort or ID checks, tests 1-6 pass but test 7 fails because Sprint 12's fetch completes after Long Cycle's, overwriting the cycleDetail. The simplest solution is to handle this in the test itself—after clicking Long Cycle and seeing CycleDetail, if it shows stale data like Sprint 12, click Long Cycle again to refresh. I can apply the same pattern to the `loginAndGoToCycleDetail` helper and tests 4 and 7. I'm checking whether calling `.abort()` on an already-resolved promise could throw an error, and looking at Redux Toolkit's implementation to understand how `AbortablePromise` handles this. Let me reconsider whether the ID check approach alone might be sufficient instead of relying on abort cleanup.\n\nLooking back at the test failures, the ID check prevented `cycleDetail` from rendering in tests 4 and 7, but without the check it passed. The issue seems to be that when a new cycle is selected, the old fetch completes first and fails the ID check, so the new fetch needs to complete before `cycleDetail` gets set. Let me trace through what happens: when the test clicks a new cycle, it dispatches `setSelectedCycleId`, React re-renders, the `useEffect` fires and dispatches `fetchCycleIssues` with the new ID, and that fetch should eventually complete and pass the ID check.\n\nBut here's what I'm realizing—when the Sprint 12 fetch completes, it dispatches a fulfilled action that hits the reducer's ID check and fails silently without updating state. The new cycle's fetch then completes and passes the check. The real problem might be that the fulfilled action from the old fetch is still being processed even though it doesn't update