[2026-03-16T00:42:34.828Z] {"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01NnmMnbuZ8nbxrkCEfodZx1","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Same issue! 5 passed, 2 failed. Tests 4 and 7 still fail with `cycle-detail` not visible.\n\nThis is very strange. The requestId approach should work because:\n1. When the test clicks the empty cycle, the useEffect dispatches fetchCycleIssues(emptyCycleId)\n2. The pending action sets latestCycleIssuesRequestId to the new request's ID\n3. Sprint 12's response (from auto-selection) arrives → requestId doesn't match → skipped\n4. Empty cycle's response arrives → requestId matches → cycleDetail set\n\nBut tests 4 and 7 consistently fail regardless of which approach I use (ID check, abort, requestId). This means the issue isn't about stale responses at all. The issue is that `fetchCycleIssues` for the new cycle NEVER DISPATCHES or NEVER COMPLETES.\n \nThe pattern is clear now—tests 1-3 and 5-6 all pass because they use the pre-existing Sprint 12 cycle, while tests 4 and 7 fail because they create cycles through the API directly and then click them. When the click handler fires and dispatches the cycle selection, the subsequent useEffect that should fetch issues for that new cycle either isn't triggering or isn't completing properly.\n\nI'm tracing through the request lifecycle: the initial page load auto-selects Sprint 12 and starts fetching its issues with one request ID, then when the test clicks the new cycle, a second fetch request gets dispatched with a different ID. The first request completes but gets ignored because its ID doesn't match the latest one, and the second request either fails or gets stuck somehow.\n\nNow I'm wondering if the click itself is even working—the test waits up to 30 seconds for the cycle row to be clickable, which should trigger the selection handler and dispatch the Redux action to update the selected cycle ID. But maybe the element isn't becoming clickable in time, or something else is preventing the dispatch from happening.\n\nActually, looking at the error context more carefully: the cycle row has the `[active]` class applied, which means the click DID fire and Redux was updated with the new selected cycle ID. So the real issue is that `cycleDetail` never gets populated—either the `fetchCycleIssues` effect didn't trigger when `selectedCycleId` changed, the API call failed silently, or the reducer rejected the response because the `requestId` didn't match what was stored in `latestCycleIssuesRequestId`.\n\nThe `requestId` mismatch could happen if another pending action fires between the new cycle's pending and fulfilled actions—like if the auto-selection logic triggers another fetch after the test clicks, causing the `latestCycleIssuesRequestId` to get overwritten before the first fetch completes. But since `createAsyncThunk` dispatches the pending action synchronously, the timing should be straightforward: the pending action fires immediately when the thunk is dispatched, so the `requestId` should match unless there's a race condition I'm missing.\n\nActually, the real issue might be that the `useEffect` isn't firing at all when the cycle ID changes, which would mean `fetchCycleIssues` never gets dispatched for the new cycle. I'm going to try a different debugging approach—instead of chasing Redux state changes, let me directly monitor the network requests to see if the API call is even being made. But actually, I think the simplest fix is to bypass all this Redux complexity for test 7 by either navigating directly to a URL with the cycle ID or using `page.evaluate` to dispatch the action manually.\n\nWait, I need to check if the app even has a URL-based route for cycle details. If not, the cleanest solution is to ensure the auto-selection fetch completes before the test clicks the new cycle—so I'll wait for the cycle detail to fully load, go back to the list, then click the Long Cycle to avoid the race condition.\n\nWriting test navigation logic...\n\nI'm realizing the issue—when I go back to the cycle list, the auto-selection mechanism triggers again and re-selects Sprint 12, creating a race condition. Instead, I should wait for the auto-selection and its data fetch to fully complete before clicking on the Long Cycle, so there's only one fetch happening at a time rather than competing requests.\n\nBut wait, looking at the component structure, when CycleDetail is displayed, the CycleList is completely hidden, so I can't click on another cycle row while viewing the detail view.\n\nI'm considering dispatching Redux actions directly from the test using `page.evaluate` to set the selected cycle before auto-selection kicks in, but that would require the Redux store to be exposed on the window object, which the app doesn't do by default.\n\nThe real insight is that the cycle list disappears once CycleDetail renders, so I need to either click before that happens or find a way to select the Long Cycle after the auto-selection completes. Let me try waiting for the auto-selection to finish and then programmatically selecting the Long Cycle instead of relying on clicking it from the list.\n\nActually, the core problem is a race condition in the Redux logic—when multiple cycle fetches happen, the last one to complete overwrites the others. Instead of trying to time clicks perfectly, I should fix the thunk itself to handle this by checking if the response is still relevant before updating the state.\n\nWriting race condition handler... I'm setting up browser console logging in the test to capture any messages, then adding debugging to the reducer to figure out why the `requestId` approach isn't working as expected.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1710,"cache_read_input_tokens":122341,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1710,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":8,"service_tie