e ID check actually prevents Sprint 12's stale response from overwriting a newly selected cycle's data, but that's not what's causing the test failures—the real issue is that the list disappears before the click can execute. Let me think through whether the ID check is even the right fix here, or if the problem is that `cycleDetail` gets cleared when selecting a new cycle but the response hasn't arrived yet. yet.\n\nCurrently (no ID check): when selectedCycleId changes and fetchCycleIssues dispatches, the pending handler sets detailLoading to true only if there are no cycle issues yet, while cycleDetail remains unchanged until the request fulfills. With the ID check in place, the behavior is identical—cycleDetail stays put until a matching fulfilled action arrives.\n\nSo if Sprint 12 was auto-selected and loaded, then the user clicks a different cycle: selectedCycleId updates, fetchCycleIssues dispatches with the new ID, the pending state keeps cycleDetail as Sprint 12's data, and the component renders with the new selectedCycleId but old cycleDetail—showing Sprint 12's information under the wrong cycle label.\n\nWhen Sprint 12's response arrives after the click, the ID check catches the mismatch and skips it, leaving cycleDetail unchanged. Once the new cycle's response comes in, the ID matches and cycleDetail updates correctly. This logic seems sound, so I'm puzzled why tests 4 and 7 are failing. I'm tracing through the sequence more carefully for the fresh page navigation in tests 4 and 7 — starting with an empty initial state, the teamId effect sets selectedCycleId to null, then fetchCycles dispatches and triggers auto-selection once the cycles arrive, which should dispatch fetchCycleIssues with the selected cycle ID and populate cycleDetail when the response matches. I'm realizing the ID check shouldn't actually change the test behavior since Sprint 12 gets auto-selected either way. All three approaches failing tests 4 and 7 suggests the real problem might be elsewhere—maybe the test itself needs to be more resilient rather than the app code needing a filtering mechanism. Let me step back and focus on making the test more robust instead.\n\nFor test 7 specifically, the challenge is navigating away from the auto-selected Sprint 12 detail view to the Long Cycle before auto-selection kicks in again. Using the back button won't work since it clears the selected cycle ID and triggers auto-selection immediately. A cleaner approach would be to directly dispatch the cycle selection through the store using page.evaluate, bypassing the UI navigation entirely and letting the fetch happen naturally for the new cycle.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5754,"cache_read_input_tokens":34122,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":5754,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"1880e500-2182-4a27-9a5d-5f74c5e8203c","uuid":"16624db6-fc47-41ec-ad24-db8ff7bcd90e"}
