int12) dispatches\n6. Test click arrives: setSelectedCycleId(newCycleId)\n7. fetchCycleIssues effect: fetchCycleIssues(newCycleId) dispatches\n8. Sprint12 response gets skipped because meta.arg doesn't match the current selectedCycleId, but the newCycleId response applies correctly.\n\nScenario B: Auto-selection fires after test click\n1. setSelectedCycleId(null) dispatches\n2. React re-renders, CycleList shows\n3. Test click arrives: setSelectedCycleId(newCycleId)\n4. Auto-selection effect checks !selectedCycleId, finds it's false, and skips.\n\nBoth scenarios should work, but the real problem is that cycleDetail never gets populated. In Scenario A, the fetchCycleIssues effect runs for Sprint12 first, but I need to trace through what happens when that response arrives.\n\nThe teamId effect only resets selectedCycleId when teamId changes, so that's not interfering after the initial setup.\n\nNow I'm checking the pending handler for fetchCycleIssues to see if it's clearing cycleDetail when the request starts, which could explain why the detail view isn't showing.\n\nActually, I think I see the issue—when I click back and setSelectedCycleId(null) fires, it clears cycleDetail entirely. Then the auto-selection kicks in and sets selectedCycleId back to Sprint12, but cycleDetail only gets populated when a payload comes through, not just from the ID change. So if I'm clicking to a different cycle before the Sprint12 response arrives, that response gets skipped because the meta.arg doesn't match the current selectedCycleId, leaving cycleDetail null.\n\nBut once the newCycleId response comes back and matches the current selectedCycleId, cycleDetail should finally populate and render. The real question is whether the newly created cycle is even fetchable through the API, or if test 4 is navigating directly to the cycles page and expecting either the cycle list or detail to show up. I need to trace through the timing more carefully. After the page navigates, React mounts and the teamId effect runs first, setting selectedCycleId to null since there's no stored value. Then fetchCycles dispatches, and the test waits for either the cycle list or cycle detail to appear. When the test clicks on the cycle row, it triggers setSelectedCycleId with the new cycle ID and dispatches fetchCycleIssues, but I'm wondering if the auto-selection logic might have already fired and triggered a fetch for a different cycle before the test's click even registered.\n\nLooking at the response handling, I see the issue now: when the Sprint12 response arrives, it checks if the meta.arg matches the current selectedCycleId, and since the test has already changed selectedCycleId to newCycleId, that response gets skipped. Then the newCycle response arrives and matches, so it gets processed correctly.\n\nBut there's a race condition I'm missing—if the Sprint12 response arrives *before* the test clicks to change cycles, then cycleDetail gets set and CycleDetail renders instead of CycleList. When the test then tries to click the cycle-row element, it's no longer in the DOM because CycleDetail is showing, so the click keeps retrying for the full 30-second timeout until it fails at the expect statement. So cycleDetail remained null even though the fulfilled handler should've populated it. Either the fetch request failed, the guard condition filtered out the response because selectedCycleId changed, or selectedCycleId got overwritten after I clicked. The auto-selection logic shouldn't re-trigger since selectedCycleId is already set, but I need to check if fetchCycleIssues fired multiple times when selectedCycleId changed.\n\nWhen selectedCycleId switches from Sprint12 to newCycleId, the effect triggers a new fetch for newCycleId while the previous Sprint12 request is still in flight. And after clicking back, setSelectedCycleId(null) clears both cycleDetail and cycleIssues, which then sets detailLoading to true on the next pending state—but that shouldn't affect whether cycleDetail renders since it depends on both selectedCycleId and cycleDetail being truthy. Let me check if there's a Replay recording to trace what actually happened.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1725,"cache_read_input_tokens":59529,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1725,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"1880e500-2182-4a27-9a5d-5f74c5e8203c","uuid":"df6b4fba-2354-46a0-8ff4-646111642c6a"}
