[2026-03-16T01:16:55.664Z] {"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01UGxFvimVmgKpoXGHV4Dys5","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"For test 7, the issue is:\n1. Page loads, auto-selection picks Sprint 12\n2. fetchCycleIssues(Sprint12) dispatches  \n3. Test clicks Long Cycle\n4. fetchCycleIssues(LongCycle) dispatches\n5. Race: if Sprint12's response arrives AFTER LongCycle's response, it overwrites cycleDetail with Sprint 12 data\n\nThe fix: before navigating, intercept ALL cycle-issues API responses. When the Long Cycle's response arrives, let it through. For any other cycle-issues response, also let it through but make sure it arrives BEFORE the Long Cycle's. OR, simply block Sprint 12's response.\n\n\nActually, I think the cleanest approach is to skip the helper entirely and instead set up route interception before navigating—blocking any cycle-issues responses except for the Long Cycle. That way, when Sprint 12 auto-selects and tries to fetch its data, the response gets blocked, keeping the CycleList visible. Then when the test clicks Long Cycle, that fetch goes through unblocked.\n\nSetting up the route handler... Delaying the first request by 10 seconds would work but makes the test too slow, and I'm not even sure it guarantees the ordering I need. The \"Route is already handled\" error from before came from mixing `route.fetch()` with `route.fulfill()`, so using `route.continue()` should avoid that, but it still doesn't solve the fundamental problem. I think the better approach is to skip the helper function entirely and manually set up the navigation flow.\n\nLet me try a different strategy for test 7: log in via API to get the token, set up route interception to block the first cycle-issues request, navigate to the cycles page, wait for the list to load, click Long Cycle, then remove the interception and wait for the detail to load. But actually, aborting the request might cause the rejected thunk to set a detailError, which would break the test. Maybe I should just let the route continue normally but ensure the click happens before the auto-selection response arrives—but that's inherently racy and unreliable. What if I just disable auto-selection entirely for this test instead?\n\nI could pre-populate the Redux store with a selectedCycleId before navigating, but I don't have direct store access. Or I could use localStorage or a query parameter to pre-select the cycle, but neither of those mechanisms exist in the app yet. Let me go back to the abort approach—even if the first request fails and shows an error, the CycleList should still render, so I can click Long Cycle and its fetch should succeed with the correct data.\n\nActually, I need to check what happens when fetchCycleIssues is rejected...\n\nThe rejected handler sets detailError, but that doesn't affect the condition that decides whether to show CycleDetail or CycleList, so CycleList stays visible. When I click Long Cycle, it dispatches a new fetchCycleIssues request that isn't intercepted, the response arrives, and CycleDetail renders with the right data. Though I should be careful—aborting the first request might cause issues at the network layer.\n\nWhen `route.abort()` is called in Playwright, it causes the browser's fetch to fail with a TypeError, which the thunk catches and rejects. I need to verify how the thunk handles this error—whether it uses `rejectWithValue` for network errors or falls back to default rejection behavior.\n\nLooking at the thunk implementation, if `fetch()` throws an uncaught error, createAsyncThunk serializes it and dispatches a rejected action with the error in `action.error` rather than `action.payload`. The rejected handler then accesses this error to update the state.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":874,"cache_read_input_tokens":83299,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":874,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"1880e500-2182-4a27-9a5d-5f74c5e8203c","uuid":"9316c585-bec7-4a07-8b06-0714a45efd0e"}
