[2026-03-16T01:19:13.562Z] {"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01V8JrJDiE7274RnmN1rbBm1","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The page snapshot shows:\n- CycleList is visible\n- The \"Long Cycle\" button has `[active]` attribute (was clicked)\n- But CycleDetail never appeared (cycleDetail is null)\n\nSo selectedCycleId is set to Long Cycle's ID, but cycleDetail stays null. This means:\n1. The click happened successfully\n2. `fetchCycleIssues(LongCycleId)` was dispatched  \n3. But the response either never arrived or was rejected\n\nSince I aborted the FIRST cycle-issues request (Sprint 12's), the Long Cycle's request should be the SECOND one. \n\nNow I'm wondering if the route handler is still intercepting the Long Cycle fetch even though it should only abort the first request. Let me trace through the sequence: the route handler gets installed with abort-first logic, I navigate to cycles, Sprint 12 auto-selects and triggers the first fetch which gets aborted, then the rejection handler fires and sets the error. When I click Long Cycle, selectedCycleId updates, but I need to verify whether the effect actually re-runs and dispatches a new fetch, or if something's preventing that.\n\nThe effect depends on selectedCycleId changing from Sprint12 to LongCycleId, so it should fire. But the timing matters here—the fetch dispatch happens asynchronously after the click, which means it could be getting intercepted by the route handler that's still active. The page.unroute() call happens after the click, so there's a window where the new fetch request might still hit the abort logic. potentially after unroute. Let me trace:\n\nStep 5: Click → handleSelectCycle(LongCycleId) → dispatch setSelectedCycleId(LongCycleId)\nStep 6 (after step 5): page.unroute('**/api/cycle-issues**')\n\nThen React re-renders (async). The fetchCycleIssues effect sees selectedCycleId changed. It dispatches fetchCycleIssues(LongCycleId). The fetch() call goes to the network. Since unroute was called, no interception.\n\nBut wait - is there a timing issue? The `page.unroute()` is awaited. Does it complete before the React effect fires?\n\nPlaywright's `page.unroute()` removes the route handler. It should complete almost instantly. The React effect runs after the browser's paint, which happens after the event handler completes. So by the time the effect fires and triggers the fetch, the route should already be removed and the request goes through normally. The synchronous dispatch happens first, then unroute completes, then React's render cycle kicks in with the effect running after—so the fetch happens against an unintercepted network.\n\nThe newly created cycle has no issues, so the API should return a valid response with empty issues and burndown data. But I'm wondering if there's something about how the thunk handles the abort. When I abort the first request, createAsyncThunk wraps the rejection. The fetch throws an AbortError, which gets caught by createAsyncThunk's internal try-catch, so it shouldn't cause unhandled rejections in the browser.\n\nLooking at the page snapshot, I see three cycles listed: Sprint 12, Empty Chart Cycle from test 4, and Long Cycle.\n\nThe Long Cycle button shows as active, but CycleDetail isn't rendering—which means cycleDetail is null. I'm wondering if the `fetchCycleIssues` effect is even running. What if `selectedCycleId` was already set to LongCycle from somewhere else?\n\nActually, I think I see the issue now. After auto-selection sets `selectedCycleId` to Sprint12 and that fetch gets aborted, the test clicks Long Cycle to change `selectedCycleId` to LongCycle. The effect should trigger because the dependency changed. But what if React batches the auto-selection's state update and the click handler's state update together so quickly that they collapse into a single update?\n\nIf that happens, only LongCycle ends up in state, the Sprint12 fetch never fires at all, and when the Long Cycle fetch runs, it becomes the \"first\" request—which then gets aborted. That's the bug. I need to stop aborting based on request order and instead specifically abort requests for Sprint12's cycle ID, but I don't have that ID upfront so I need to check the API response to figure out which cycle ID corresponds to Sprint12.\n\nActually, I can extract the cycleId directly from the URL query parameter and compare it against the new cycle's ID—if they match, let it through; otherwise block it. This way I'm specifically targeting Sprint12's request instead of blindly aborting the first one. Now I'm wondering if I still need to unroute afterward and whether there are other edge cases to handle.\n\nLet me think through the flow: keep the route active and allow Long Cycle requests while blocking others, then unroute once assertions pass or the cycle detail loads. The tricky part is that aborting Sprint 12's request rejects the thunk and sets a detailError, but the fetchCycleIssues effect should still trigger when selectedCycleId changes to the Long Cycle. As long as my check correctly identifies the Long Cycle's request, it'll go through. If auto-selection doesn't happen at all, no request fires until the test clicks, and that first request would be the Long Cycle's—which passes the check and succeeds.\n\nReact batching the dispatches would mean only one request fires anyway, so that's fine. I'll remove the unroute call from the route handler itself since it's already filtering correctly, and just add cleanup unroute at the test's end.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2846,"cache_read_input_tokens":89133,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2846,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"1880e500-2182-4a27-9a5d-5f74c5e8203c","uuid":"97981