ng Cycle request.\n\nLet me verify whether this is consistently reproducible or a one-off. I should also double-check that `newCycle.id` is actually being set correctly from the API response—the POST endpoint returns the UUID, and the GET request should be matching against that same ID. The route interception is checking if the cycleId matches `newCycle.id`, so if there's a mismatch there, that could explain why the request isn't being intercepted properly.\n\nThe server is returning a 500 error even though the route handler is correctly identifying and allowing the request through, which means something on the backend is failing. I should check the dev-server console for error logs. This could be a Neon cold-start issue—if the Sprint 12 request was aborted before reaching the server, the Long Cycle request might be the first one to actually hit the Neon endpoint, and a cold start could cause a timeout or failure.\n\nActually, wait—the test creates the cycle through a direct API call that hits the same dev-server, so the endpoint should already be warm. Let me reconsider what's happening when Playwright aborts a request. When `route.abort()` is called, the request never actually reaches the server at all—it's blocked at the browser level. So the Sprint 12 cycle-issues request never made it to the dev-server, but the cycles list fetch and the Long Cycle fetch did reach it. Since I set up the route handler before `page.goto()`, all cycle-issues requests go through it, but the `/api/cycles` endpoint isn't intercepted, so that loads fine. The sequence is: Sprint 12 auto-selects, triggers a cycle-issues request that gets aborted before reaching the server, then the Long Cycle is clicked and its request goes through.\n\nNow when the Long Cycle request actually reaches the server, it's returning a 500 error. The dev-server should still be warm from the earlier `createCycleViaApi` call, which also uses the same `getSql()` connection mechanism. The newly created cycle has no issues associated with it, so the SQL query returns an empty array for `issueIds`.\n\nThe burndown calculation falls back to `['__none__']` when there are no issues, and the members query runs independently. The cycle itself was created successfully with start and end dates of August 1-28, 2026. When the cycle-issues endpoint reads these dates back from the database, it normalizes them to ISO string format regardless of whether they come back as Date objects or strings.\n\nI'm going in circles trying to debug this. Let me just call the endpoint directly with the cycle ID to reproduce the 500 error, or skip the route interception altogether and handle the timing issue differently. Actually, I should verify the flow: `createCycleViaApi` runs in the test's Node.js context using fetch, returns the parsed response with the cycle ID, and that same ID gets used in the route handler which also runs in the test context.\n\nLet me just make a direct API call to the cycle-issues endpoint to see what the actual 500 error is.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1715,"cache_read_input_tokens":98740,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1715,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"1880e500-2182-4a27-9a5d-5f74c5e8203c","uuid":"74122687-4be5-4fd5-a247-cd3dc8ac0c19"}
